  There is also an asymmetric regime. Give the along-$0$ variables $sans(Y)_1 \, sans(Z)_0 \, sans(z)_0 \, sans(P)_0$ arbitrarily large damping and temporarily remove every product containing one of them. At $sigma = h_1 = 0$, with $h_0$ bounded, the remaining arrows are exactly
  $ sans(z)_1 arrow.r sans(Y)_0 \, #h(2em) sans(z)_1 arrow.r sans(P)_1 \, #h(2em) sans(z)_1 arrow.r sans(P)_gamma . $
  For example, the $sans(P)_1$ source contains $sans(Y)_1$, which has been removed, rather than $sans(Y)_0$; and $sans(d)_1 = 0$. The retained trace and shift equations have no unsuppressed source returning to $sans(z)_1$.
  Give each paired current multiplier one, the trace currents multiplier $Lambda^2$, and the $sans(P)$ currents multiplier $epsilon.alt_P^2$. Take $Lambda$ large and $epsilon.alt_P$ small, depending only on $M \, delta$ and the bounded low coefficients. Both displayed limiting quadratic forms then have norm at most $delta \/ 4$ in the rescaled energy. After those choices, continuity on the bounded low coefficient set gives a fixed threshold $epsilon_(*)$: the whole form has cost at most $delta \/ 2$ when $sigma \, h_0 \, h_1 lt.eq epsilon_(*)$, and the retained long-direction form has that cost when $sigma \, h_1 lt.eq epsilon_(*)$.
  Use the common Lipschitz weight
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(5.19)")
   $ frak(w)_T = exp { - 2 delta \( u + v \) - 2 K_0 \( u + B_(upright("cut")) \)_(+) - 2 K_0 min \( t \, S_0 \) } . $ <dn:high-weight>]
  Uniform tail smallness from (5.11) permits $B_(upright("cut")) \, S_0$ independent of large $T$ such that the all-small regime holds for $t > S_0 \, u < - B_(upright("cut"))$ and the asymmetric regime holds for $t > S_0 \, - B_(upright("cut")) lt.eq u lt.eq u_2$. Its directional damping is, almost everywhere,
  $ - L_k log frak(w)_T = 2 delta + 2 K_0 upright(bold(1))_({ k = 0 \, u > - B_(upright("cut")) }) + K_0 upright(bold(1))_({ t < S_0 }) . $
  If $C_(upright("bd"))$ bounds the complete rescaled form, the products containing an along-$0$ factor are absorbed by $K_0 > C \( C_(upright("bd")) + C_(upright("bd"))^2 \/ delta \)$. In $t < S_0$ both directions have this damping. The choice order is
  $ \( Lambda \, epsilon.alt_P \) \, quad epsilon_(*) \, quad \( B_(upright("cut")) \, S_0 \) \, quad K_0 . $
  In particular choosing $K_0$ does not change a previously selected tail threshold. All constants use only the dependencies stated in the Lemma.
  Integration gives positive null and slice fluxes bounded by the initial fluxes. The tilted sides of (5.1) have the same outgoing signs. Since $u lt.eq u_2$ and $v lt.eq C \( T + 1 \)$,
  $ 1 gt.eq frak(w)_T gt.eq exp { - C delta T - 2 K_0 \( u_2 + B_(upright("cut")) \)_(+) - 2 K_0 S_0 - C delta } . $
  Removing the weight loses an arbitrarily small exponential rate. Initial integration over intervals of length $O \( T \)$ costs only a polynomial. In particular the transverse fluxes satisfy
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(5.20)")
   $ sup_(y_j) parallel sans(Y)_i + sans(d)_i parallel_(L^2 \( upright(d) y_i \)) + sup_(y_i) parallel sans(Z)_i parallel_(L^2 \( upright(d) y_j \)) lt.eq e^(o \( T \)) . $ <dn:high-transverse>]
  The intervals here are the available intersections with the initial partial slab. Every past coordinate segment stays inside it and reaches $t = 0$, because the side defining functions are nondecreasing along both $L_0 \, L_1$.
  The weighted angular fluxes are now controlled. Unweighted fields and ordinary longitudinal jets remain to be recovered. Recover the former by transport, rather than dividing the $sans(Z)$ estimate by $sigma$. The damped trace equation and (5.14) give
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(5.21)")
   $ sans(z)_i \( y \) lt.eq  & e^(o \( T \)) e^(- kappa \( y_i + y_j \))\
 & + C'_M integral_(- y_j)^(y_i) e^(- kappa \( y_i - r \)) h_i \( sans(Y)_i + sans(d)_i + h_i sans(P) \) \( r \, y_j \) thin upright(d) r . $ <dn:high-trace-convolution>]
  The homogeneous $C_M h_i$ factor has bounded directional integral. To see explicitly how the trace enters unweighted transport, fix $y_j$ and integrate (5.21) in its terminal variable. Reversing the integration triangle gives
  $ integral_(- y_j)^(y_i) sans(z)_i \( s \, y_j \) thin upright(d) s lt.eq e^(o \( T \)) + C'_M / kappa integral_(- y_j)^(y_i) h_i \( sans(Y)_i + sans(d)_i + h_i sans(P) \) \( r \, y_j \) thin upright(d) r . $
  The first transverse flux in (5.20) bounds the $sans(Y)_i + sans(d)_i$ integral by Cauchy–Schwarz, with only a polynomial interval factor. Since $h_i$ is uniformly bounded, $h_i^2 lt.eq C h_i$. The Hodge estimate for $sans(R)_i$ consequently gives
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(5.22)")
   $ integral_(- y_j)^(y_i) \( sans(z)_i + sans(R)_i \) \( r \, y_j \) thin upright(d) r lt.eq e^(o \( T \)) + C_M integral_(- y_j)^(y_i) h_i \( r \, y_j \) sans(P) \( r \, y_j \) thin upright(d) r . $ <dn:high-pure-integrals>]
  The norm inequalities for metric and torsion transport are
  $ sans(P)_gamma \( y \) & lt.eq e^(o \( T \)) + C_M integral_(- u)^v \( sans(R)_1 + h_1 sans(P)_gamma \) \( u \, r \) thin upright(d) r \,\
sans(P)_i \( y \) & lt.eq e^(o \( T \)) + C_M integral_(- y_j)^(y_i) \( sans(Y)_i + sans(R)_i + sans(d)_i + h_i sans(P) \) \( r \, y_j \) thin upright(d) r . $
  Substitution of (5.22) and the transverse flux therefore gives
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(5.23)")
   $ sans(P) \( y \) lt.eq e^(o \( T \)) + C''_M sum_i integral_(- y_j)^(y_i) h_i \( r \, y_j \) sans(P) \( r \, y_j \) thin upright(d) r . $ <dn:high-volterra>]
  Multiply by $e^(- C Phi_T \( y \))$ and take a supremum. Along either path,
  $ integral_(- y_j)^(y_i) h_i \( r \, y_j \) e^(- C \( Phi_T \( y \) - Phi_T \( r \, y_j \) \)) thin upright(d) r lt.eq C^(- 1) . $
  Choose $C > 4 C''_M$ and absorb the two integrals. The uniform bound on $Phi_T$ proves $sans(P) \, sans(z)_i lt.eq e^(o \( T \))$. (5.22) now bounds the pure-direction integral of $sans(R)_i$ by $e^(o \( T \))$ as well.
  We record the angular order obtained by the last transport step. The pointwise $sans(P)$ bound controls $gamma$ in $H^(M + 1)$ and $xi_i$ in $H^M$. Tame differentiation of angular curvature and connection therefore gives
  $ \| a "Sym" nabla xi_j \|_(M - 1) + \| a K_gamma gamma \|_(M - 1) + \| a xi_j^(⊗ 2) \|_(M - 1) lt.eq C_M q sans(P) . $
  Commuting the cross equations with $D$ through order $M - 1$ gives top homogeneous coefficient $C_M h_j$ on the $chi_i \, ell_i$ norms. The other shape and shift terms are bounded by $C_M h_i \( sans(R)_j + sans(d)_j \) + C_M h_i h_j sans(P)$. Their integrals in direction $j$ are $e^(o \( T \))$ by (5.20), (5.22). Gronwall thus gives pointwise $H^(M - 1)$ bounds for $chi_i \, ell_i$. Finally $\[ partial_v \, D^r \] b = 0$ and the shift source has $H^M$ norm at most $C_M q sans(P)$; its norm derivative adds only $C_M h_1 \| b \|_M$. Integration gives a pointwise $H^M$ bound for $b$. Thus the order-$M$ current gives pointwise $H^(M - 1)$ bounds for all relative fields, and running it with $M gt.eq A + 1$ supplies an angular target order $A$. The shift step uses the pointwise torsion bound: the transverse integration direction of the $sans(Z)_1$ flux in (5.20) is $0$.
  We next specify the longitudinal induction and its finite angular buffer. Fix a largest longitudinal order $n_parallel$ and a desired final angular order $A_(upright("out")) gt.eq 5$, and put
  $ A_k = A_(upright("out")) + 2 \( n_parallel - k \) \, #h(2em) 0 lt.eq k lt.eq n_parallel . $
  At level $k$ the assertion is an $e^(o \( T \))$ bound in reference $H^(A_k)$ for every ordered transport jet of the relative fields listed in the Lemma with exactly $k$ longitudinal derivatives: use $L_i$ on scalars and $frak(L)_i$ on angular tensors. For $b$ we also keep its ordinary longitudinal jets at that order. All earlier levels retain their larger $H^(A_ell)$ bounds. Run the preceding angular argument with $M gt.eq A_0 + 1$ to establish level zero. The first pure jets in the bootstrap supply low coefficient bounds; the high-angular induction begins at $k = 1$ from this level-zero conclusion.
  For $i eq.not j$, the exact identities needed for the induction are
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(5.24)")
   $ \[ frak(L)_j \, frak(L)_i \] = cal(L)_(X_(i j)) \, #h(2em) X_(i j) = a \( xi_j - xi_i \)^sharp \,\
frak(L)_i gamma = 2 chi_i \, #h(2em) L_i log cal(A) = ell_i + kappa \, #h(2em) frak(L)_i \( xi_0 + xi_1 \) = upright(d) ell_i . $ <dn:longitudinal-identities>]
  For $k gt.eq 1$, Raychaudhuri, torsion transport, and angular curvature variation give the reductions
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(5.25)")
   $ L_i^k tau_i & = L_i^(k - 1) \( ell_i tau_i - \| chi_i \|^2 \) \,\
frak(L)_i^k xi_j & = frak(L)_i^(k - 1) \( beta_i + tau_i xi_i \) \,\
frak(L)_i^k xi_i & = upright(d) L_i^(k - 1) ell_i - frak(L)_i^k xi_j \,\
L_i^k K_gamma & = L_i^(k - 1) \( "div" beta_i - tau_i K_gamma \) \,\
 & = sum_(r = 0)^(k - 1) frak(L)_i^r cal(L)_(X_(i j)) frak(L)_i^(k - 1 - r) . $ <dn:longitudinal-reductions>]
  The scalar commutator has the same formula with $X_(i j)$ acting by directional differentiation. Opposite shape and lapse derivatives use their cross equations. The variation of the angular Levi-Civita connection under $frak(L)_i gamma = 2 chi_i$ is
  $ \( frak(L)_i nabla \)^C zws_(A B) = gamma^(C D) \( nabla_A chi_(i \, B D) + nabla_B chi_(i \, A D) - nabla_D chi_(i \, A B) \) \, $
  and every derivative of $a$ retains its $a$ factor times relative fields. Thus a reduction to a preceding longitudinal level adds at most two angular derivatives: the largest costs are $"div" beta_i$ and the outer $nabla$ on the torsion reduction. The buffer $A_k$ covers these costs.
  Set $U_(i \, k) = frak(L)_i^k chi_i$ and $V_(i \, k) = L_i^k ell_i$. Commuting the cross equations and using (5.25) gives
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(5.26)")
   $ frak(L)_j U_(i \, k) & = - 1 / 2 tau_j U_(i \, k) + U_(i \, k) chi_j + chi_j U_(i \, k) + cal(F)_(i \, k) \,\
L_j V_(i \, k) & = - U_(i \, k) dot.op chi_j + cal(G)_(i \, k) . $ <dn:pure-top-system>]
  Here $cal(F)_(i \, k) \, cal(G)_(i \, k)$ contain only longitudinal levels below $k$ and their buffered angular derivatives. In particular the first reduction in (5.25) replaces the differentiated term $- 1 / 2 \( L_i^k tau_i \) chi_j$ by lower levels. This is why that term is absent from the displayed top system. The new lapse jet does not occur on its right side.
  For completeness, commute the first equation of (5.26) with $D$ in increasing angular order. For $r gt.eq 4$ the tame product and shift commutator inequalities give
  $ frac(upright(d), upright(d) y_j) \| U_(i \, k) \|_r & lt.eq C_r h_j \| U_(i \, k) \|_r + C_r cal(C)_(j \, r) sum_(ell < r) \| U_(i \, k) \|_ell + \| cal(F)_(i \, k) \|_r \,\
cal(C)_(j \, r) & = 1 + \| gamma \|_(r + 1) + \| gamma^(- 1) \|_(r + 1) + \| chi_j \|_r + \| B_j \|_(r + 1) . $
  This norm inequality is understood in its regularized form at zero. At the first four angular orders the low $H^4$ shape and $H^5$ shift bounds give the same top coefficient directly. At higher orders $cal(C)_(j \, r)$ multiplies already controlled lower angular orders of $U_(i \, k)$; it is a source in this subsidiary induction. It is $e^(o \( T \))$ by level zero. The lower longitudinal induction bounds $cal(F)_(i \, k)$ using $A_ell gt.eq A_k + 2 \( k - ell \)$. Consequently Gronwall exponentiates only the uniformly bounded directional integral of $C_r h_j$. Finite products of earlier $e^(o \( T \))$ bounds and integration over length $O \( T \)$ remain $e^(o \( T \))$. After $U_(i \, k)$ is controlled, the second equation has the same transport commutators and the known source $U_(i \, k) dot.op chi_j$, so it controls $V_(i \, k)$ in the same way.
  The reductions also cover mixed words. In an ordered word of length $k$ acting on a torsion, apply its innermost transport derivative first: the torsion equation applies to the opposite torsion, and the torsion-sum identity gives the derivative of the same-index torsion. The remaining $k - 1$ operators then act on $beta_i \, ell_i$ and products of fields. Expanding their angular commutators uses the same two-derivative buffer. The metric and relative lapse words reduce in the same way by (5.24). For a mixed shape or lapse word, commute a cross derivative inward; the commutator in (5.25) has shorter words, and the cross equation reduces the resulting innermost derivative. This gives the already controlled pure or lower levels. For the shift, differentiate its exact direction-$1$ equation $k$ times in $u$ and integrate to obtain $partial_u^k b$. Conversion of its source from transport to ordinary derivatives uses only longitudinal orders below $k$ of $b$: expanding $partial_u = frak(L)_0 - cal(L)_b$ on tensors, or $partial_u = L_0 - b dot.op upright(d)$ on scalars, differentiates $b$ at most $k - 1$ times. Each replacement adds at most one angular derivative, which is covered by the same buffer. Mixed shift jets follow directly from its equation. Finally $q^(- 1) partial_v b = cal(A) gamma^(- 1) \( xi_1 - xi_0 \)$ is algebraic. This completes level $k$ and the conversion to ordinary jets.
  The required initial pure jets are finitely many entry jets. They can also be expressed using tangential $x$ jets and the cross equations on the noncharacteristic cylinder, where $q = q_0 > 0$. For an output order $n$, take $n_parallel = n$ and $A_(upright("out")) = max \( n \, 5 \)$, then choose $M gt.eq A_0 + 1$ as above. Include the derivatives defining $K \, beta$ and the initial cross identities and take their finite maximum as $r \( n \)$. All sources in the argument are polynomial in the finitely many earlier high norms; only low directional integrals enter an exponential. This gives the asserted uniform dependence and leaves $r \( n \)$ independent of later frequency-accuracy choices. The Lemma follows. ◻
]

=== 5.3 The unweighted low error system <the-unweighted-low-error-system>

Write $Delta f = f' - macron(f)$. The following sizes on a sphere use angular norms in uniformly equivalent low geometry; for the paired energies we use the primed metric exactly:

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(5.27)")
 $ Y_i & = \| Delta beta_i \|_0 \, & Z_i & = sigma \| \( Delta Q_i \, Delta m_i \) \|_0 \, & z_i & = \| Delta w_i \|_1 \,\
N_i & = \| Delta chi_i \|_0 + \| Delta ell_i \|_0 \, & D_b & = \| Delta b \|_1 \, & R_i & = \| Delta chi_i \|_1 \,\
P & = \| Delta gamma \|_1 + \| Delta log cal(A) \|_0 + \| Delta xi_0 \|_0 + \| Delta xi_1 \|_0 . $ <dn:low-sizes>]

A squared sum below means the sum of the component squares, with each component assigned its indicated transport direction. This convention matters for the components of $P$. Set

$ V = P + D_b + sum_i \( N_i + Y_i + Z_i + z_i \) \, #h(2em) H_s = sigma + h_0 + h_1 . $

#thm([Proposition 5.3.])[
  (Quadratic low error).  #emph[For every $eta_2 > 0$, the input loss $eta$ and the support fraction $nu$ may be chosen sufficiently small, with the finite base orders fixed, so that on every existing bootstrap subslab 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(5.28)")
 $ sup_(0 lt.eq t lt.eq L_T) parallel \( Delta gamma \, Delta log cal(A) \, Delta b \, Delta chi_i \, Delta ell_i \, Delta xi_i \) parallel_(L^2 \( I_T \( t \) times bb(S)^2 \)) lt.eq A_T^2 e^(eta_2 T) . $ <dn:low-conclusion>]

 The supremum is restricted to available slices. At the same times, 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(5.29)")
 $ sum_i parallel Y_i + Z_i + z_i parallel_(L^2 \( I_T \( t \) \)) + parallel \( nabla Delta gamma \, nabla Delta b \) parallel_(L^2 \( I_T \( t \) times bb(S)^2 \)) lt.eq A_T^2 e^(eta_2 T) . $ <dn:low-auxiliary-conclusion>]

]
] <prop:dn-low-error>

#proof[
  We give the operator differences first, to check derivative counts before estimating the currents. Put $h_(A B) = Delta gamma_(A B)$ in the next two displays only. The difference of angular connections is exactly
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(5.30)")
   $ C^C zws_(A B) := \( Gamma' \)^C zws_(A B) - \( macron(Gamma) \)^C zws_(A B) = 1 / 2 \( gamma' ""^(- 1) \)^(C D) \( macron(nabla)_A h_(B D) + macron(nabla)_B h_(A D) - macron(nabla)_D h_(A B) \) . $ <dn:connection-difference>]
  Thus $\| C \|_0 lt.eq C \| Delta gamma \|_1$. For a barred one-form $f$,
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(5.31)")
   $ \( "div"' - overline("div") \) f & = \( \( gamma' ""^(- 1) \)^(A B) - \( macron(gamma)^(- 1) \)^(A B) \) macron(nabla)_A f_B - \( gamma' ""^(- 1) \)^(A B) C^D zws_(A B) f_D \,\
\( "curl"' - overline("curl") \) f & = 1 / 2 \( \( epsilon.alt' \)^(A B) - macron(epsilon.alt)^(A B) \) \( upright(d) f \)_(A B) . $ <dn:operator-differences>]
  The analogous divergence formula on two-tensors has one connection difference for each covariant slot. No second derivative of $h$ occurs. Against $macron(beta)_i$ or $macron(chi)_i$, these differences cost $C h_i P$, since their required low angular derivatives are $O \( h_i \)$; against bounded barred torsion they cost $C P$.
  The exact torsion-sum identity gives
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(5.32)")
   $ \| Delta log cal(A) \|_1 lt.eq C P \, #h(2em) \| Delta cal(A) \|_1 lt.eq C P . $ <dn:H-one>]
  For the trace conversion, use the exact identity
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(5.33)")
   $ Delta tau_i = cal(A)' Delta w_i + \( Delta cal(A) \) macron(w)_i \, #h(2em) \| Delta tau_i \|_1 lt.eq C \( z_i + h_i P \) . $ <dn:trace-conversion>]
  The trace of $Delta chi_i$ in the primed metric is $Delta tau_i - \( gamma' ""^(- 1) - macron(gamma)^(- 1) \) macron(chi)_i$. Writing $C_(upright("inv")) = gamma' ""^(- 1) - macron(gamma)^(- 1)$, the full shape Hodge datum is exactly
  $ "div"' Delta chi_i - upright(d) "tr"' Delta chi_i = Delta beta_i - \( "div"' - overline("div") \) macron(chi)_i + upright(d) \( C_(upright("inv")) : macron(chi)_i \) . $
  Apply the primed trace-free divergence estimate, including the undifferentiated tensor norm to cover its kernel, and then the primed one-form div–curl estimate. (5.31) and (5.33) yield
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(5.34)")
   $ R_i lt.eq C \( Y_i + N_i + z_i + h_i P \) \, #h(2em) \| Delta xi_j \|_1 lt.eq C \( sigma^(- 1) Z_i + P \) . $ <dn:difference-hodge>]
  For the latter, the divergence data are $Delta K_gamma - Delta m_i$ and the curl datum is $Delta c_i$, plus the displayed coefficient differences on $macron(xi)_j$. Precisely,
  $ "div"' Delta xi_j = Delta K_gamma - Delta m_i - \( "div"' - overline("div") \) macron(xi)_j \, #h(2em) "curl"' Delta xi_j = Delta c_i - \( "curl"' - overline("curl") \) macron(xi)_j . $
  These first-order elliptic constants need only the uniform low Lipschitz geometry. The estimate does not assert an unweighted bound for the torsion derivative by $Z_i$.
  When subtracting a transport equation, keep the actual operator:
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(5.35)")
   $ frak(L)'_i f' - macron(frak(L))_i macron(f) & = frak(L)'_i Delta f + cal(L)_(Delta B_i) macron(f) \,\
L'_i f' - macron(L)_i macron(f) & = L'_i Delta f + Delta B_i dot.op upright(d) macron(f) . $ <dn:transport-differences>]
  For a one-form, the last Lie term consists of $Delta B_i dot.op partial macron(f) + \( partial Delta B_i \) * macron(f)$. It costs $C h_i D_b$ on $macron(beta)_i \, macron(chi)_i$, $C D_b$ on bounded barred torsion, and $C D_b$ on $macron(Q)_i \, macron(m)_i$ before their $sigma$ weight. On $macron(ell)_i = - kappa + O \( h_i \)$ it costs $C h_i D_b$, since the constant $- kappa$ has zero angular derivative.
  ===== 5.3.1 The paired currents. <the-paired-currents.>
  Writing $k = Delta K_gamma$ and $c = Delta c_i$, the primed principal part of the difference system is
  $ frak(L)'_j Delta beta_i = a' / 2 \( upright(d) k + J' upright(d) c \) + F_i \, #h(2em) L'_i Delta Q_i = \( "div"' \, "curl"' \) Delta beta_i + G_i \, #h(2em) L'_i Delta m_i = G_i^m . $
  There are no angular derivatives in the last principal equation. Use the currents with densities
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(5.36)")
   $ e_(Y_i) = 1 / 2 \| Delta beta_i \|_(gamma')^2 quad upright("in direction ") j \, #h(2em) e_(Z_i) = a' / 4 \( \| Delta Q_i \|^2 + \| Delta m_i \|^2 \) quad upright("in direction ") i . $ <dn:pair-densities>]
  The ratio of scalar to one-form weight is exactly $a' \/ 2$. Since $upright(d)$ and $J' upright(d)$ have adjoints $- "div"'$ and $- "curl"'$, their principal contribution, integrated on the full sphere, is
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(5.37)")
   $ - 1 / 2 integral_(bb(S)^2) chevron.l Delta beta_i \, \( upright(d) a' \) thin k + J' \( upright(d) a' \) thin c chevron.r_(gamma') thin upright(d) mu_(gamma') . $ <dn:principal-cancellation>]
