   $ D \( macron(g) \) - D \( g \) = - 1 / 2 A_T lambda^2 cos \( lambda v \) \( p_0 \)_(A B) + O \( A_T lambda e^(o \( T \)) \) + O \( A_T^2 lambda^P e^(o \( T \)) \) . $ <at:leading-signal>]
  The last term comprises quadratic template products in the smooth relative-field expression (6.5). Choose $zeta$ small enough that this term is $o \( A_T \)$. The exact-minus-template error from 15, at the finite derivative orders in (6.5), is also $o \( A_T \)$; multiplication by the individual $e^(o \( T \))$ coefficient bounds preserves this conclusion.
  By (4.4), on the cosine-good set the leading norm is at least $1 / 4 A_T lambda^2 e^(- o \( T \))$ uniformly throughout $cal(Q)_T$. Its ratio to the linear lower-order error tends to infinity, since $lambda e^(- o \( T \)) arrow.r oo$, and its ratio to $A_T$ also tends to infinity. This proves (6.4). The argument compares two curvature tensors at common numerical coordinate positions; the background curvature is never presumed small or bounded independently of $T$. ◻
]

=== 6.3 Canonical terminal states and finite persistence <canonical-terminal-states-and-finite-persistence>

Let $O in cal(B)$ be the fixed test ball from 11, with $overline(O) subset cal(N)$ for the chosen observation neighborhood. Choose a closed concentric subball $O_0 subset.double O$ such that

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(6.8)")
 $ mu_d \( O_0 \) > .95 mu_d \( O \) . $ <at:trim>]

Such a choice follows by monotone convergence of the smooth positive density. Choose a smaller closed interval $J_0 subset.double J$ of positive length. For every late level $v gt.eq v_0$ reached by the tube, let $cal(H)_v$ be the background hitting map of $O_0$, where $v_0$ is the fixed late starting level. It records the canonical state

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(6.9)")
 $ z = \( u \, theta \, pi_u \, P_1 \, P_2 \) . $ <at:terminal-state>]

The original seed-to-covector identification uses $w mapsto h \( w \, dot.op \)$ on $Sigma$. Thus $mu_d$ is exactly the canonical section measure, not a position-only measure.

#thm([Proposition 6.3.])[
  (Matched seeds and finite observations).  #emph[For $v in T + J_0$, use the same numerical states (6.9) in $g_T$ on its future unit shell. Their backward geodesics give maps $Psi_(T \, v) : O_0 arrow.r O$ with 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(6.10)")
 $ sup_(v in T + J_0) parallel Psi_(T \, v) - "incl" parallel_(C^1) arrow.r 0 . $ <at:seed-closeness>]

 Write $delta_T$ for the left side of (6.10). Each map is injective and preserves the corresponding section measure: $Psi_(T \, v)^(*) mu_(d_T) = mu_d$ on $O_0$. In particular $cal(H)_v \( O_0 \)$ has canonical volume $mu_d \( O_0 \)$ for every such $v$.]
  #emph[Put 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(6.11)")
 $ r_T = e^(- kappa T) \, #h(2em) F = \( partial_u \, r_T^(- 1) partial_v \, partial_(theta^1) \, partial_(theta^2) \) . $ <at:frame>]

 For $g$ and $g_T$ on the larger observation box, the Gram matrices in $F$ and their inverses are uniformly bounded and nondegenerate. Their ordinary coordinate jets through order thirty are $e^(o \( T \))$. The selected unit tangent is bounded in $F$, all state momenta lie in one bounded box, and 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(6.12)")
 $ c r_T thin upright(d) u thin upright(d) v thin upright(d)^2 theta lt.eq upright(d) "vol"_g \, med upright(d) "vol"_(g_T) lt.eq C r_T thin upright(d) u thin upright(d) v thin upright(d)^2 theta . $ <at:volume>]

]
  #emph[For each fixed finite $T$ and each $eta_(upright("map")) > 0$, these observations persist for all data in a sufficiently small relative weighted smooth neighborhood of $d_T$, with signal lower bound $A_T$ in place of $2 A_T$. The persisted common coordinates need not be optical. The canonical section volume, injective maps into $O$, and frame and density bounds persist. Their backward maps satisfy 
$ sup_(v in T + J_0) parallel hat(Psi)_(T \, v) - Psi_(T \, v) parallel_(C^1) < eta_(upright("map")) \, #h(2em) sup_(v in T + J_0) parallel hat(Psi)_(T \, v) - "incl" parallel_(C^1) lt.eq delta_T + eta_(upright("map")) . $

 The neighborhood may depend on $T$ and $eta_(upright("map"))$.]
] <prop:matched-seeds>

#proof[
  The unit mass shell in double-null variables reads
  $ p = - \( pi_u + b^A P_A \) > 0 \, #h(2em) 2 p pi_v + a \( 1 + gamma^(A B) P_A P_B \) = 0 . $
  Thus the reduced $v$-Hamiltonian is
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(6.13)")
   $ cal(H) = - pi_v = frac(a \( 1 + gamma^(A B) P_A P_B \), 2 p) . $ <at:reduced-H>]
  The same expression with primed fields defines the new future unit root at the identical numerical terminal state. The background tube keeps $p$ in a compact positive interval and the other momenta bounded. Since $b_T - b arrow.r 0$ uniformly, the new $p_T = - \( pi_u + b_T^A P_A \)$ remains bounded away from zero. Choosing the root with $dot(v) = p_T \/ a_T > 0$ fixes its future orientation.
  Write $V$ for the background Hamiltonian vector field. On the common late state tube this field and its first two state derivatives are bounded by $a e^(o \( v \))$, by 6. This bound is integrable over the late $v$ interval. Differentiating (6.13) shows that the new field and its first two state derivatives differ by an absolutely exponentially small quantity, uniformly on that tube. Indeed $a_T = q cal(A)_T$ retains its factor $q$ under ordinary differentiation; the denominators are powers of $p_T$ separated from zero; and all relative coefficient differences and their needed jets are exponentially small by 13 and 15. Fixed higher background factors cost $e^(o \( T \))$, which is absorbed by that exponential.
  The terminal state difference is exactly zero. Backward Gronwall on a late interval of length $O \( T \)$ therefore gives state differences tending to zero. For first variations the equation is $J' = D_z V thin J$. Its coefficient difference is bounded by the small difference of vector-field derivatives plus $\| D_z^2 V \|$ times the already small state difference. The latter background bound is integrable. A second Gronwall estimate therefore gives uniform $C^1$ convergence of the backward maps. The background forward hitting map also has a uniform derivative bound: if $v_0$ is the fixed late starting level, then
  $ parallel D_w cal(H)_v parallel lt.eq parallel D_w cal(H)_(v_0) parallel exp #h(-0.16667em) (integral_(v_0)^v parallel D_z V \( s \) parallel thin upright(d) s) lt.eq C . $
  Composing the terminal-state estimates with $cal(H)_v$ therefore preserves their $C^1$ convergence in the original seed variables. The strict tube and construction margins prevent exit during this comparison. Below any fixed late $v$ the remaining flow tubes to $cal(S)$ and then $Sigma$ are compact and transverse. Smooth finite-flow dependence handles their hitting maps; protected agreement makes their metrics exactly the background metrics for sufficiently large $T$. This proves (6.10) and, by the margin $O_0 subset.double O$, places the new seeds in $O$.
  Here is the exact volume statement. On the cotangent bundle, let
  $ omega = upright(d) pi_u and upright(d) u + upright(d) pi_v and upright(d) v + upright(d) P_A and upright(d) theta^A . $
  On the unit Hamiltonian shell its restriction has the geodesic flow direction as kernel. A single-valued transverse hitting map therefore preserves the restrictions of $omega$ to two sections: flowing by a variable time only adds multiples of its kernel to tangent vectors. On $v = upright("constant")$ this restriction is exactly
  $ omega_v = upright(d) pi_u and upright(d) u + upright(d) P_A and upright(d) theta^A \, $
  independently of the shell root $pi_v$. Its volume is $upright(d) u thin upright(d)^2 theta thin upright(d) pi_u thin upright(d)^2 P$. Apply this to the background hit and to the new backward hit. It gives $Psi_(T \, v)^(*) mu_(d_T) = mu_d$ exactly. The hits are single-valued on these compact transverse flow tubes; uniqueness also gives injectivity. Alternatively, in the ball coordinates (6.10) makes the map $upright("Id") + f$ with $parallel D f parallel < 1 \/ 2$, which is injective on the convex closed ball. Nothing in this argument assumes injectivity of the projection from seed states to observation positions.
  At an observation state the unit tangent satisfies
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(6.14)")
   $ dot(u) = frac(1 + gamma^(A B) P_A P_B, 2 p) \, #h(2em) r_T dot(v) = frac(r_T p, a) \, #h(2em) dot(theta) = b dot(u) + gamma^(- 1) P \, $ <at:tangent-components>]
  and likewise for $g_T$. On the whole observation box, $u$ and $v - T$ are bounded, so $a \/ r_T = q_0 cal(A) e^(- kappa \( u + v - T \))$ is bounded above and below. These formulas prove the tangent bounds in $F$. The Gram matrix has the block form
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(6.15)")
   $ G_F = mat(delim: "(", gamma \( b \, b \), - a \/ r_T, - b^C gamma_(C B); - a \/ r_T, 0, 0; - gamma_(A C) b^C, 0, gamma_(A B)) \, #h(2em) det G_F = - \( a \/ r_T \)^2 det gamma . $ <at:gram>]
  The low field bounds give bounded entries and a determinant bounded away from zero, hence a bounded inverse. The same holds for $g_T$. All ordinary derivatives of $a \/ r_T$ retain this bounded relative factor, and the other required field jets are $e^(o \( T \))$ by the individual estimates. This proves the thirty-jet assertion on the larger box, not just on the hitting positions. Finally $sqrt(\| det g \|) = a sqrt(det gamma)$ proves (6.12).
  We finish with the finite persistence assertion. For a fixed $T$ the closures of the observation box, a small enlargement, and all backward flow tubes just used are compact subsets of the smooth attached development with an interior margin. The compact-subset Cauchy stability and geometric uniqueness of #cite(label("KerrCTwo")), also used in the closed-test proof of #cite(label("KerrCOne")) , proof, supply corresponding compact coordinate witnesses for all sufficiently close smooth data. Require closeness in the finite $C^k$ norms needed here. Since $T$ is fixed, it can be arbitrarily accurate even in the rescaled frame $F$. Preserve the Gram and inverse bounds, the thirty derivative bounds (for example allowing additive error at most one), the density comparison, and the curvature margin, so that
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(6.16)")
   $ \| D \( hat(g)_T \) - D \( g \) \| > A_T quad upright("when") quad \| cos \( lambda v \) \| gt.eq 1 \/ 2 . $ <at:persisted-signal>]
  The common coordinates for $hat(g)_T$ need not retain double-null form. To define its terminal state solve $1 / 2 hat(g)_T^(- 1) \( pi \, pi \) = - 1 / 2$ for $pi_v$ with the other six numbers fixed as in (6.9). At the original terminal state the derivative with respect to $pi_v$ is $U^v > 0$. On the fixed compact state set it is separated from zero, so the implicit function theorem supplies the same smooth simple future root for close metrics. The level-$v$ section remains transverse to this flow. Smooth backward-flow dependence lets us impose $sup_(v in T + J_0) parallel hat(Psi)_(T \, v) - Psi_(T \, v) parallel_(C^1) < min { eta_(upright("map")) \, eta_0 }$, where a fixed $eta_0 > 0$ preserves injectivity and the image margin in $O$. The restriction of the canonical symplectic form to that section is still $omega_v$, regardless of optical gauge. Its transported volume is therefore exactly the new initial seed measure. All statements hold for this fixed experiment, with no assertion of a data-neighborhood radius uniform in $T$. ◻
]

== 7 Averaged holonomy and the category argument <ho:section>

We turn the finite curvature difference of 19 into an obstruction to a square-integrable connection. The estimate is averaged over four position variables and three momentum variables. The loops themselves remain in the smooth development. In particular, no restriction of the weak ambient connection to a prescribed curve is required. Sbierski developed holonomy obstructions at Lipschitz regularity #cite(label("Sbierski2022")) and a related nonsymmetric obstruction based on integrated curvature #cite(label("Sbierski2026")). Here the ambient hypothesis controls an integral over positions; averaging must therefore precede any curvewise transport estimate. The stopping argument below establishes the needed chart control for the sampled loops.

=== 7.1 Canonical parameters and the observation frame <canonical-parameters-and-the-observation-frame>

Write

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.1)")
 $ r = r_T = e^(- kappa T) \, #h(2em) l_T = e^(- kappa T \/ 16) \, #h(2em) A_T = e^(- kappa T \/ 4) . $ <ho:scales>]

The observation coordinates are $\( u \, v \, theta^1 \, theta^2 \)$, and the frame used throughout this section is

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.2)")
 $ F_0 = partial_u \, #h(2em) F_1 = r^(- 1) partial_v \, #h(2em) F_2 = partial_(theta^1) \, quad F_3 = partial_(theta^2) . $ <ho:frame>]

For each fixed experiment, $r$ is a constant on the entire observation box. Thus the four fields in (7.2) commute.

Let $O_0 subset.double O$ be the closed trimmed ball from 20, chosen so that

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.3)")
 $ mu_d \( O_0 \) > .95 mu_d \( O \) . $ <ho:trim>]

For $v in T + J_0$, where $J_0$ is a fixed compact interval of positive length, let $cal(P)_(T \, v)$ be the set of terminal states $\( u \, theta \, pi_u \, P \)$ obtained by following the background seeds in $O_0$ to that level. The full parameter set and its measure are

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.4)")
 $ cal(P)_T = { \( v \, u \, theta \, pi_u \, P \) : v in T + J_0 \, med \( u \, theta \, pi_u \, P \) in cal(P)_(T \, v) } \, #h(2em) upright(d) nu_T = upright(d) v thin upright(d) u thin upright(d)^2 theta thin upright(d) pi_u thin upright(d)^2 P . $ <ho:parameter-measure>]

The three momentum labels range in a fixed bounded box $Q subset bb(R)^3$. Here and below, measures may be completed without changing any estimate.

We recall why these are canonical measures, also for the nearby metrics used later. On the future unit shell of $cal(H) \( x \, pi \) = 1 / 2 g^(alpha beta) \( x \) pi_alpha pi_beta$, a transverse $v$ section has a simple root for $pi_v$ because $partial_(pi_v) cal(H) = dot(v) > 0$. On that section the pullback of the symplectic form is

$ upright(d) pi_u and upright(d) u + sum_(A = 1)^2 upright(d) P_A and upright(d) theta^A . $

The Hamiltonian vector field is the kernel of the symplectic form restricted to the shell. Its transverse hitting maps consequently preserve this two-form and its canonical volume, the absolute value of its third exterior power divided by $3 !$. The corresponding section on $Sigma$, pulled back by $w mapsto h \( w \, dot.op \)$, is precisely $mu_d$. Hence

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.5)")
 $ integral_(cal(P)_(T \, v)) upright(d) u thin upright(d)^2 theta thin upright(d) pi_u thin upright(d)^2 P = mu_d \( O_0 \) \, #h(2em) nu_T \( cal(P)_T \) = \| J_0 \| mu_d \( O_0 \) . $ <ho:slice-volume>]

This uses injectivity of transverse phase-space hitting maps; it makes no injectivity assertion about their projection onto positions.

The following argument applies separately to the background metric and to any persisted finite experiment satisfying 20. Denote either metric temporarily by $g$ and put $G_(i j) = g \( F_i \, F_j \)$. On an observation box containing the positions in (7.4) with a fixed margin, we have

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.6)")
 $ parallel G parallel + parallel G^(- 1) parallel lt.eq C \, #h(2em) sum_(\| beta \| lt.eq 30) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] parallel partial^beta G parallel + parallel partial^beta G^(- 1) parallel #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] lt.eq Q_T \, #h(2em) Q_T = e^(o \( T \)) . $ <ho:gram-assumptions>]

All derivatives here are ordinary coordinate derivatives. The selected future unit tangent $U$ at every parameter in $cal(P)_T$ has bounded $F$ components. Constants in this section may depend on the fixed observation tube, $O$, and the chart bound $B$, but not on $T$. Finite products of subexponential bounds are absorbed into a new $Q_T$ as in 3.

If $g_(upright("coord"))$ is the ordinary coordinate matrix, the constant frame change gives $det G = r^(- 2) det g_(upright("coord"))$. Thus

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.7)")
 $ upright(d) "vol"_g = r sqrt(\| det G \|) thin upright(d) u thin upright(d) v thin upright(d)^2 theta \, #h(2em) c r lt.eq sqrt(\| det g_(upright("coord")) \|) lt.eq C r . $ <ho:volume-density>]

For an optical metric these bounds follow directly from $a \/ r asymp 1$, the bounded angular metric and shift. Formula (7.7) also holds for the nearby metrics without requiring the observation coordinates to remain optical.

#thm([Lemma 7.1.])[
  (Comparison at a good base point).  #emph[Suppose an extension chart has bounded metric and inverse metric, and the components of $U$ are bounded both in that chart and in $F$. Then the matrix $M$ with columns $F_i$ in chart coordinates and its inverse are bounded by a constant depending only on these bounds and (7.6).]
] <ho:base-comparison>

#proof[
  For any Lorentzian Gram matrix $G$ and unit timelike column $U$, set
  $ K \( G \, U \) = G + 2 \( G U \) \( G U \)^(sans(t)) . $
  The associated form is positive definite: writing a vector as $a U + Z$ with $g \( U \, Z \) = 0$ gives $a^2 + g \( Z \, Z \) > 0$ unless the vector vanishes. The determinant lemma and $U^(sans(t)) G U = - 1$ give the exact identity
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.8)")
   $ det K \( G \, U \) = det G thin \( 1 + 2 U^(sans(t)) G U \) = - det G . $ <ho:positive-determinant>]
  Bounded $G \, G^(- 1) \, U$ give an upper eigenvalue bound for $K$ and a positive lower bound for its determinant. Its smallest eigenvalue is therefore bounded below by the determinant divided by the cube of the upper eigenvalue bound. Apply this in both bases. If $g_c$ is the chart matrix and $U_c = M U_F$, then
  $ K \( G \, U_F \) = M^(sans(t)) K \( g_c \, U_c \) M . $
  The two uniform positive definite comparisons bound both $M$ and $M^(- 1)$. In particular, bounded Lorentzian Gram matrices alone are not being used to bound a Lorentz boost. ◻
]

=== 7.2 Finite jets of coordinate rectangles <finite-jets-of-coordinate-rectangles>

The scale choices can be read from the estimates proved below. With a stopped edge-integral cutoff $R$, position averaging will leave exceptional measure $O \( \( r R^2 \)^(- 1) \)$, while the extracted quadratic coefficient will be bounded by $O \( r R \/ l_T \) + Q_T l_T^16$. Taking

$ R = r^(- 17 \/ 32) \, #h(2em) l_T = r^(1 \/ 16) \, #h(2em) A_T = r^(1 \/ 4) $

gives exceptional measure $O \( r^(1 \/ 16) \)$ and curvature errors $O \( r^(13 \/ 32) \) + Q_T r = o \( A_T \)$. The power $l_T^16$ comes from an eighteenth-order remainder after extracting a quadratic coefficient.

Let

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.9)")
 $ cal(X) = { partial_(theta^1) \, partial_(theta^2) \, partial_(theta^1) + partial_(theta^2) } . $ <ho:directions>]

For a position $x$ in the parameter set, $X in cal(X)$, and $0 lt.eq l lt.eq 17 l_T$, take the coordinate rectangle with consecutive displacements $l partial_v \, l r X \, - l partial_v \, - l r X$. Denote its holonomy in the based frame $F \( x \)$ by $H_(x \, X) \( l \)$. Every rectangle lies in the enlarged observation box for large $T$, because its ordinary coordinate displacement is $O \( l \)$. Its velocity in $F$, on each unit-parameter edge, is $l r$ times a fixed bounded column. The two ordinary side lengths have different scales, although both directions have this same $F$-component scale. 2 illustrates these two scales. The quadratic coefficient of the loop holonomy recovers the curvature block $D$. We will extract that coefficient from the eighteen values at $l = 0 \, l_T \, dots.h \, 17 l_T$; a bound for the eighteenth derivative controls the interpolation remainder at the required $o \( A_T \)$ scale.

